  "query_preview": "..\nTonight through Friday: An upper trough will continue to pull\neast and away from the Inland NW tonight and be replaced by a\nmid level dry slot. This will lead to a quick end with shallow\nconvection with clearing skies. Light winds combined with\nclearing will allow for strong radiational cooling tonight with\nlows mainly in the mid 30s to mid 40s. The coldest spots such as\nRepublic and Deer Park have the potential for patchy frost. The\ndry air lingers over the area through Friday morning for a\ncontinued dry forecast. Friday afternoon a mid level wave drops\ndown from the northwest over southeast British Columbia,\nsouthern Alberta, and western Montana along the Rocky Mountain \nDivide; clipping northeast Washington and the Idaho Panhandle.\nThere is about 20 percent of model solutions that track this\nfeature further west, giving a broader chance for showers and\nthunderstorms to Northeast Washington into the ID Panhandle. But\nfor now the main threat of showers and thunderstorms will be\nfound over the mountains of NE WA and the ID Panhandle.\n\nSaturday through Wednesday: There is good agreement that an\nupper ridge will bring a warming trend into early next week. The\nridge then begins to flatten into Tuesday and Wednesday.\nAlthough the initial flattening on Tuesday will result in an\nadditional boost to temperatures as breezy winds develop. High\ntemperatures Tuesday will reach the upper 80s and 90s with\nmoderate HeatRisk for much of the region. Dry and breezy \nconditions Tuesday and Wednesday will lead to elevated fire \nweather conditions in dry grasses as well. JW",
